arser with xref=this parses object 5 as a stream\n5. makeStream calls getEntry(5)\n6. The entry for 5 has flags=0 (from resize)\n7. setFlag(Parsing, true) does: flags |= mask → reads flags (0) → writes flags = 1\n8. This should be fine for MSAN since flags was initialized to 0 by resize\n\nSo all entries created by resize have flags=0, which is initialized. MSAN shouldn't complain.\n\nThe only way to trigger this is if there's an entry whose flags was NEVER initialized. Let me look for such a path.\n\nWhat about the `parseEntry` function in the mainXRefEntriesOffset path?\n```cpp\nif ((!xRefStream) && mainXRefEntriesOffset) {\n    if (!parseEntry(mainXRefEntriesOffset + 20*i, &entries[i])) {\n        error(...);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`parseEntry` sets flags=0 ONLY on success. If it fails, the entry might not have flags initialized... but wait, the entry was already initialized by resize (which was called during XRef construction). So even if parseEntry fails, the entry still has flags=0 from resize.\n\nHmm, what about this scenario: entries[i] was created by resize with flags=0. Then in readXRefTable, parseEntry is called and sets flags=0 again. Then later, some other operation modifies the entry without going through the proper path...\n\nActually, wait. Let me look at a VERY specific scenario. What if:\n1. The XRef is a hybrid: it has a table with /XRefStm pointing to an XRef stream\n2. The table declares some entries (flags=0 from parseEntry/resize)\n3. The XRef stream declares additional entries\n4. But during the XRef stream reading, if readXRefStream has a bug...\n\nHmm, let me look at the readXRefTable function when it processes /XRefStm:\n\n```cpp\n// Look for an XRefStm entry in the trailer dict\nobj2 = trailerDict.dictLookupNF(\"XRefStm\");\nif (obj2.isInt()) {\n    pos2 = obj2.getInt();\n    readXRef(&pos2, followedXRefStm, xrefStreamObjsNum);\n}\n```\n\nThis calls readXRef again for the hybrid stream. During this call, the Parser is created with xref=nullptr