ng it (even without `= nullptr`), or maybe MSAN tracks it differently on the stack. Let me try with some more variations of the PDF.\n\nOr... maybe the issue is completely different. What if the binary was built from a state where `entry` IS initialized to nullptr (because the fix is at HEAD), but there's STILL an MSAN bug with the `flags` field? In that case, entry at the end would be nullptr, no crash there. But at the beginning, when xref IS valid:\n\n```cpp\nif (xref && (entry = xref->getEntry(objNum, false))) {\n    if (!entry->getFlag(XRefEntry::Parsing) || ...) {\n        entry->setFlag(XRefEntry::Parsing, true);  // reads flags here!\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIf the entry's flags is uninitialized, this would trigger MSAN.\n\nSo maybe the real bug IS about entry->flags being uninitialized in certain entries! And the fix commit `89fccf45` is NOT the right fix for this variant. The real fix might be elsewhere (in XRef stream section reading).\n\nIf that's the case, I need to create a PDF where:\n1. The XRef uses streams (so readXRefStreamSection is used)\n2. After XRef is read, an object is fetched\n3. The object is a stream\n4. makeStream calls getEntry and gets an entry whose flags is uninitialized\n\nBut wait, resize DOES set flags=0. Unless... there's a case where an entry is set by readXRefStreamSection BEFORE resize sets its flags?\n\nThat can't happen because resize is called first:\n```cpp\nbool XRef::readXRefStream(Stream *xrefStr, Goffset *pos) {\n    ...\n    int newSize = obj.getInt();\n    if (newSize > size) {\n        if (resize(newSize) != size) {  // resize first!\n            ...\n        }\n    }\n    ...\n    readXRefStreamSection(xrefStr, w, first, n);  // then fill sections\n}\n```\n\nresize is called BEFORE readXRefStreamSection. So all entries have flags=0.\n\nI'm really stuck. Let me just try more creative PDFs to see what triggers the MSAN error.\n\nWhat if I try a PDF that causes the readXRef to FAIL (because of an invalid XRef stream structure) and then fall through to constructXRef? During constructXRef, when it finds an object that's a stream, it creates a Parser with allowStreams=false. But what if AFTER constructXRef, a stream object is fetched?\n\nAfter constructXRef rebuilds the XRef table:\n- resize is called → flags=0\n- entries are filled with type/offset/gen\n- Then fetch() is called on a stream object\n- Parser is created with xref=this, allowStreams=true\n- makeStream is called\n- getEntry returns the entry (flags=0 from resize)\n- setFlag(Parsing, true): reads flags (=0) → writes flags = 1\n- This should NOT trigger MSAN because flags IS initialized (to 0)\n\nOK so the only viable path for MSAN is the uninitialized pointer `entry`. My PoC SHOULD work if the binary has the bug. Let me try something: instead of making the XRef a stream, let me make the XRef a table but have it point to a stream object that has issues.\n\nWait, actually I just realized: in the readXRef function for XRef streams, the call is:\n```cpp\nif (obj = parser->getObj(), !obj.isStream()) {\n    goto err1;\n}\n```\n\nThis CALLS makeStream (via getObj). If makeStream returns nullptr (due to an error), obj.isStream() is false, and we goto err1. If makeStream succeeds, obj is a stream and we proceed.\n\nFor MSAN to trigger, makeStream needs to reach the `if (entry)` line at the end. This means makeStream must NOT return early.\n\nThe early returns in makeStream (vulnerable version) are:\n1. `if (entry already Parsing) return nullptr;` - won't happen since entry is uninitialized and we skip the first if block\n2. `if (!(str = lexer->getStream())) return nullptr;` - after skipToNextLine\n3. `if (strict) return nullptr;` - strict is false\n4. `if (!lexer->getStream()) return nullptr;` - second check\n5. `if (unlikely(length < 0)) return nullptr;`\n6. `if (unlikely(pos > LLONG_MAX - length)) return nullptr;`\n7. `if (strict) return nullptr;` - second strict check\n\nFor my PDF:\n- Length = 4 (valid, >= 0)\n- pos = position aft